Top level reads as a sentence:<br/><pre>function onboardUser(input: RawInput) {
  const data = normalizeInput(input);
  const account = createAccount(data);
  sendWelcomeEmail(account);
  return account;
}</pre>Each step is its own named function (<code>normalizeInput</code>, <code>createAccount</code>, <code>sendWelcomeEmail</code>), so the step comments are now function names verified by the type checker. The WHAT-narration is gone. The WHY from step 3 moves inside the function where the decision lives:<br/><pre>function sendWelcomeEmail(account: Account) {
  // Skipped under test to avoid hitting the mail provider. See ONB-12.
  if (isTestEnv()) return;
  mailer.send(welcomeTemplate(account));
}</pre>Now the body of onboardUser reads like the original step comments, but every name is checked and reused, and the only surviving comment is the one no name could express.

